  "problem": "A communications beacon of mass $24\\,\\text{kg}$ is launched downward with an initial velocity of $4\\,\\text{m/s}$ and continues to fall vertically. Let $v(t)$ be the beacon's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after launch, with downward taken as the positive direction.\n\nThe air resistance on the beacon is proportional to its velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the beacon\u2019s velocity is $8\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $8.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the beacon are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\nFind the equation for the velocity $v(t),$ expressing your answer in terms of $t$ **only.**\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}",
